
#import "../../../_lib/kodama.typ": *

#show: kodama

#metadata((
  title: "Adic space",
))

#subtree(
  title: "Definition of an adic space",
  taxon: "section"
)[
$op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ is equipped with a topology generated by:
$
  {x mid(|) abs(t(x)) <= abs(s(x)) != 0}, t,s in A
$
or equivalently, all rational subsets $U(T/s)$ forms a topological basis. #footnote[Via replacing $t$ by $t + I^n$ for $n >> 0$ so that $(t + I^n)A subset A$ is open.] The bijection $op("Spa")(A, A^+) = op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+)$ is a homeomorphism. 

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ is a spectral topological space and the rational subsets are open constructible. See #local("/ref/Hub93Continuous.typst", text: [Continuous valuations, Theorem 3.5]).
]

We define the presheaf $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))$ on $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ via Kan extension:
$
  scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))(W) & := lim_(U(T/s) subset W) A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r) \
  scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))^+(W) & := lim_(U(T/s) subset W) A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)^+ \
  & = {f in scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))(W) mid(|) abs(f(x)) <= 1, forall x in W}
$
A Huber pair is called sheafy if $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))$ is a sheaf of topological rings, whence $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))^+ subset scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))$ is automatically a subsheaf of topological rings.

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  For any sheafy analytic Huber pair $(A, A^+)$ and rational covering $frak(U)$, which may be assumed to be finite, we have acyclicity for all $r > 0$:
  $
    H^(r)(op("Spa")(A, A^+), scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))) = H_("Čech")^(r)(op("Spa")(A, A^+), scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+)); frak(U)) = 0
  $
  See #local("/ref/KedLiu15RelativeI.typst", text: [Relative p-adic Hodge theory, I: Foundations, Theorem 2.4.23]).
]

#subtree(
  taxon: "theorem"
)[
  For any sheafy analytic Huber pair $(A, A^+)$, the functor from finite projective $A$-modules to locally finite free $scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+))$-modules is an equivalence. See #local("/ref/KedLiu15RelativeI.typst", text: [Relative p-adic Hodge theory, I: Foundations, Theorem 2.7.7]).
]

An adic space $(X, scr(O)_X, {abs(-(x))}_(x in X))$ is a topological space $X$, a sheaf of topological rings $scr(O)_X$ and a family of an equivalence class of continuous valuations $abs(-(x))$ on $scr(O)_(X, x)$ for all $x in X$ such that $(X, scr(O)_X, {abs(-(x))}_(x in X))$ is locally isomorphic to an affinoid adic space, that is:
$
  op("Spa")(A, A^+) = op("Spa")(hat(A), hat(A)^+) = (op("Spa")(A, A^+), scr(O)_(op("Spa")(A, A^+)), {abs(-(x))}_(x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)))
$
for some sheafy Huber pair $(A, A^+)$. The morphisms are maps of topologically ringed topological spaces $f : X -> Y$ such that $scr(O)_(Y, f(x)) -> scr(O)_(X, x) -> Gamma_x union {0}$ factors through $Gamma_(f(y)) union {0}$. Then the functor:
$
  (A, A^+) mapsto op("Spa")(A, A^+)
$
from sheafy complete Huber pairs to adic spaces is fully faithful, see #local("/ref/Hub94Generalization.typst", text: [A generalization of formal schemes and rigid analytic varieties, Proposition 2.1]).

The category of adic spaces forms a full subcategory of the category of pre-adic spaces, which allows non-sheafy Huber pairs.
]

#subtree(
  title: "Analytic adic spaces",
  taxon: "section"
)[
#subtree(
  title: "analytic",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  A point $x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ is analytic if there is a rational neighbourhood $U(T/s)$ such that $A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$ is Tate, or equivalently, the kernel of the valuation $abs(-(x))$ is not open. A Huber ring $A$ is analytic if every point $x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ is analytic, or equivalently, if $A^(circle circle)$ generates the unit ideal $A$.
]

#proof()[
  - If $A lr(chevron.l T/s chevron.r)$ is Tate, every pseudo-uniformizer $g$ satisfies $abs(g^(n)(x)) != 0$ for all $n$ but every neighbourhood of $0$ contains $g^n$ for $n >> 0$.
  - If the kernel of $abs(-(x))$ is not open, let $A_0$ be a ring of definition and $I$ an ideal of definition. Then there exists $f in I$ such that $abs(f(x)) != 0$, which is automatically topologically nilpotent. As a continuous valuation, there exists $m$ such that $abs(g(x)) <= abs(f(x))$ for all $x in I^m$. Let $T$ be a finite set of generator of $I^m$, then $U(T/f)$ is rational and $A lr(chevron.l T/f chevron.r)$ is Tate with pseudo-uniformizer $f$.
  - Then $x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ is analytic if and only if there exists $f in A^(circle circle)$ non-vanishing at $x$, which holds every where if and only if the discrete ring $A slash (A^(circle circle) A)$ vanishes.
]

#subtree(
  title: "affinoid field",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  An affinoid field is a complete Huber pair $(K,K^+)$ where $K$ is either a nonarchimedean field or a discrete field, and $K^+$ is an open and bounded valuation subring.
]

#subtree(
  title: "residue field",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  We can associate each $x in op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ with a universal affinoid field, the residue field $kappa(x)$ as follows. Let $frak(p)_x subset A$ be the kernel of $abs(-(x))$, $k(x)$ be the field of fractions of $A slash frak(p)_x$ and $k(x)^+ subset k(x)$ be the valuation ring. We equip $k(x)$ with valuation topology if $x$ is analytic and discrete topology otherwise. Define $kappa(x) := (k(x), k(x)^+)$.
]

A generalization $y$ of $x$ is defined as $k(x) = k(y)$ and $k(x)^+ subset k(y)^+$. Generalizations of $x$ forms a totally ordered chain and the maximal generalization corresponds to analytic points. Caution that this generalization is different from generalization in the spectral space $op("Spa")(A, A^+)$.

#subtree(
  title: "adic morphism",
  taxon: "definition"
)[
  A morphism $f : A -> B$ of Huber rings is adic if for one, or equivalently, any, choice of ring of definitions $A_0 subset A, B_0 subset B$ with $f(A_0) subset B_0$ and ideal of definition $I subset A_0$, we have $f(I) B_0$ is an ideal of definition.

  A morphism $(A, A^+) -> (B, B^+)$ of Huber pairs is adic if $A -> B$ is, or equivalently, $op("Spa")(B,B^+) -> op("Spa")(A,A^+)$ carries analytic points to analytic points.
]

#proof()[
  If $A$ is Tate, then any $f: A -> B$ is adic, see #local("/ref/Hub93Continuous.typst", text: [Continuous valuation, Proposition 3.8]).
]
- If $(A, A^+) -> (B, B^+)$ is adic, then pullback along $op("Spa")(B, B^+) -> op("Spa")(A, A^+)$ preserves rational subsets.
- If $(B, B^+) <- (A, A^+) -> (C, C^+)$ are adic, we define:
  $
    D & := B times.o_A C \
    D_0 & := B_0 times.o_A_0 C_0 \
  $
  let $I subset A_0$ be an ideal of definition, set $I D_0$ as an ideal of definition. Let $D^+$ be the integral closure of the image $B^+ times.o_(A^+) C^+$ in $D$. Then it defines a pushout in the category of Huber pairs.
]

#subtree(
  title: "Examples",
  taxon: "section"
)[
Let us study $X := op("Spa")ZZ_(p)[|T|]$ with $(p, T)$-adic topology.
- A unique non-analytic point $x_(FF_p) : ZZ_(p)[|T|] -> FF_p -> {0,1}$ on $X$.
- A unique continuous surjective map $kappa : X without {x_(FF_p)} -> [0, infinity]$ such that 
  - for all $n kappa(x) < m$, we have $abs(T(x))^n >= abs(p(x))^m$
  - for all $n kappa(x) > m$, we have $abs(T(x))^n <= abs(p(x))^m$
- The above $kappa$ satisfy the identity:
  $
    kappa(x) = (log abs(T(x)))/(log abs(p(x))) in [0, infinity]
  $
- $kappa(x) = 0$ if and only if $abs(p(x)) = 0$, that is, $abs(-(x))$ factors through $FF_(p)[|T|]$, is the $T$-adic unit disc. 
- $kappa(x) = infinity$ if and only if $abs(T(x)) = 0$, that is, $abs(-(x))$ factors through $ZZ_p$, is the $p$-adic unit disc.
- The subspace $kappa^(-1)((0, infinity])$, that is, the locus $p != 0$, is not affinoid but admits an affinoid cover $kappa^(-1)([n^(-1), infinity]) = op("Spa")QQ_(p) lr(chevron.l T, p^(-1) T^n chevron.r)$.
- An affinoid neighbouhood of $x_(FF_(p)((T)))$, for example, $kappa^(-1)([0,1])$ corresponds to a Tate ring $ZZ_(p)[|T|] lr(chevron.l p T^(-1) chevron.r) [T^(-1)]$ not containing any nonarchimedean field.

Let us study the rigid closed unit disc $X := op("Spa")(CC_(p) lr(chevron.l T chevron.r), cal(O)_(CC_(p)) lr(chevron.l T chevron.r))$.
- Type I, the classical points, for every $x in cal(O)_(CC_(p))$ the valuation $abs(f(x))$.
- Type II, the branching points, for every closed disc with $r in abs(CC_p^times) = p^QQ$:
  $
    B = overline(BB(x, r)) := {y in CC_p mid(|) abs(y - x) <= r} subset cal(O)_(CC_p)
  $
  if $f = sum_(n>=0) c_n (T - x)^n$, the valuation:
  $
    abs(f(B)) = sup_(n>=0) abs(c_n) r^n = sup_(x in B) abs(f(x))
  $
- Type III, the irrational points, for every closed disc with $r in.not abs(CC_p^times) = p^QQ$:
  $
    B = overline(BB(x, r)) subset cal(O)_(CC_p)
  $
  if $f = sum_(n>=0) c_n (T - x)^n$, the valuation:
  $
    abs(f(B)) := sup_(n>=0) abs(c_n) r^n = sup_(x in B) abs(f(x))
  $
- Type IV, the dead ends, let $overline(BB(x_1, r_1)) supset overline(BB(x_2, r_2)) supset ...$ be a descending chain of closed discs such that $inter.big_(n>=1) overline(BB(x_n, r_n)) = nothing$, then $r := inf_(n>=1) r_n > 0$, the valuation:
  $
    abs(f({overline(BB(x_n, r_n))}_(n>=1))) := inf_(n>=1) abs(f(overline(BB(x_n, r_n))))
  $
- Type V, let $B = overline(BB(x,r))$ with $r in abs(CC_p^times) = p^QQ$ corresponding to a branching point. We introduce a positive infinitesimal $epsilon$. If $f = sum_(n>=0) c_n (T-x)^n$ the valuations:
  $
    v_(B_+)(f) & := inf_(n>=0) v_(CC_p)(c_n) - n log r - n alpha \ 
    v_(B_-)(f) & := inf_(n>=0) v_(CC_p)(c_n) - n log r + n alpha \ 
  $
  These valuation concides with the irrational points if the above construction is applied to $r in.not abs(CC_p^times) = p^QQ$. Note that $B_-$ is defined for $r < 1$ but not the Gaußian point $overline(BB(0,1))$.
The Type V point $overline(BB(0,1))_+$ is the obstuction for the open unit disc $BB(0,1)$ to be closed.
]

